The root cause of the bug is the absence of a properly declared memory region for OP-TEE in the device tree, which leads to the kernel incorrectly treating the end of DDR as free space for CMA (Contiguous Memory Allocator). Since OP-TEE was expected to use this memory region but it wasn't reserved, it caused conflicts and errors when the kernel tried to access the CMA region, resulting in erroneous behavior during runtime.

